  "notes": "のそのそ describes slow, heavy, lumbering movement. It's often used for large animals like bears, or for people who are tired, sleepy, or just moving without energy.\n\nRELATED WORDS:\n- ⟦のろのろ→のろのろ：05162_noronoro⟧: slow (emphasizes annoyingly slow pace)\n- ⟦ゆっくり→ゆっくり：00910_yukkuri⟧: slowly (neutral/positive)\n- ⟦だらだら→だらだら：05843_daradara⟧: sluggishly (implies laziness)\n- ⟦とぼとぼ→とぼとぼ：05839_tobotobo⟧: trudging wearily\n\nCOMMON PATTERNS:\n- のそのそ⟦{歩|ある}く→歩く：00538_aruku⟧: to walk slowly/lumber\n- のそのそ⟦{起|お}きる→起きる：00521_okiru⟧: to get up sluggishly\n\nUSAGE:\nUnlike ⟦のろのろ→のろのろ：05162_noronoro⟧ which can imply frustration at slowness, のそのそ has a more neutral or even endearing quality, often used for animals or sleepy people.",
